\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Product Quality and Price Signaling in a Modern Context
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
October 8, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Experimental economists have always been fascinated with the recognized
 problems that arise due asymmetric information, most notably moral hazard.
 Within the framework of industrial organization, moral hazard occurs when
 both sellers' costs and buyers' values depend on product quality.
 The sellers know the quality of their product, while the quality cannot
 be directly observed by buyers.
 One possible solution to this problem involves signaling.
 Miller and Plott (1985) designed and conducted an experiment in which sellers
 were allowed to invest in costly, observable signals.
 However, I do not believe their framwork can be readily applied to today's
 modern market institutions that have arisen due to the widespread use of
 the Internet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To elaborate, buyers of goods over the Internet are able to discern some
 of the information concerning the product's quality through user reviews
 and namebrand recognition.
 Yet, they are limited in their ability to gather all relevant information
 since they unable to physcially examine the product or have face-to-face
 communication with the seller.
 The seller, on the other hand, can invest in the quality of the product,
 but he will be unable to relay a perfect signal to the buyer about the
 product due to the same reasons mentioned previously.
 Because of these issues, will sellers even attempt to signal the quality
 of their products to buyers, or will they just simply advertise their price
 instead?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In order to answer this question, I propose the following experimental design.
 Sellers possess products that are exogenously given the identification
 of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which will be known to the buyers.
 Sellers can add units of quality to their goods, which is valued by the
 buyers, but this increase in quality is not directly observed by the buyers.
 Note that there is no initial difference in quality between the two types
 of goods, yet sellers having the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 good will have a slightly lower marginal cost of adding units of quality
 to their product.
 In addition, buyers will possess a slightly higher value for products designate
d as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Yet, buyers will have an increasing valuation for higher quality products,
 thus allowing generic brands to readily compete with their name-brand counterpa
rts.
 Due to the conditions just described, the possibility of signaling exists
 in the constructed markets, even though buyers can identify the two different
 kinds of goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, like Miller and Plot (1985), a particular market experiment will proceed
 in a sequence of periods, and in each period, sellers will have only two
 units of the same grade to sell.
 For different periods, sellers will be randomly assigned the type of grades
 they would be selling with half the sellers assigned to sell 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 goods, and the other half required to sell the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 goods.
 As has been stated, sellers can add quality to a unit at a cost which depends
 upon the type of unit they are selling.
 The marginal cost will reman constant for the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generis
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 type, while the marginal cost will change for the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 good in different market experiments.
 Finally, all buyers have the same redemption schedules, which depend on
 both the kind and quality of the unit purchased; the difference in value
 between 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 goods will be held constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, there will still be some key differences between my design and
 the one utilized by Miller and Plot (1985).
 To elaborate, the quality of a product cannot be directly observed by buyers.
 Instead, sellers and buyers will be made aware that a signal of the product's
 quality will be drawn from some fixed distribution, which will be unknown
 to the two groups of subjects.
 As sellers add more units of quality to their products, then the probability
 of relaying a positive signal to the buyers will increase.
 In addition, the trading rules differ between the two designs.
 Sellers are allowed to set their own prices, but the buyers will be the
 only ones allowed to initiate communication in order to solicit price informati
on.
 The only way sellers can relay their prices to all of the buyers is to
 pay for a signal that does so.
 The price of this signal will be the same for both 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

name-brand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

generic
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 goods, and it will change between different market experiments.
 Buyers will inform the proper sellers of their intent to purchase one unit
 or both, contracts are finalized, and payoffs will be calculated.
 The prices of transactions and the actual quality of the goods will be
 made public in order for sellers to build some semblance of a reputation
 with buyers.
 To sum up, the sequence of steps for a given period is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Sellers are exogenously assigned the type of good they will be selling,
 which will be known to the buyers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Sellers submit how much units of quality to add to their products.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Sellers decide on the prices at which to sell their goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Sellers are able to purchase a pricing signal in order to relay their prices
 to all of the buyers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Buyers now have the opportunity to solicit pricing information from the
 remaining sellers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Buyers obtain the exogenously determined random signals concerning the quality
 of the products for sale.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Buyers make their purchasing decisions, inform the sellers, and transactions
 are finalized.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
After payoffs are calculated, information about the different transactions
 that occured during the most recent period is given to both buyers and
 sellers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With this experimental design, I wish to determine if sellers will actually
 add quality to their products, even if the signal is not perfect, or will
 they just pay for the pricing signal instead.
 In addition, under what conditions will pooling or separating equilibria
 arise, and which kind will be more prevelant? I also wish to analyze different
 models and their respective predictions, such as pure and partial pooling,
 and the most efficient and inefficient signaling.
\end_layout

